course: geography
lesson: 28
title: The Silk Road
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Think of passing a note along a row of desks: nobody walks the whole way, each pupil hands it to the next. The Silk Road worked like that. It was not one road but a web of routes from China through Central Asia to Persia, India and the Mediterranean, and few traders travelled the whole of it.
explain: Caravans of two-humped Bactrian camels crossed cold deserts from one oasis to the next, resting at inns called caravanserais about a day's march apart. Oasis cities such as Samarkand, Bukhara and Kashgar grew rich in between.
explain: The road carried more than silk: horses, paper, glass and spices, and also ideas such as Buddhism going to China and papermaking going west. It carried disease too: the Black Death is thought to have spread west along trade routes in the 1340s.
explain: Europa Universalis 5 passes trade from one market to the next in a chain, which is close to how the routes really worked. But at its start in 1337 the Mongol peace that had made the roads safe was already breaking into rival khanates, and after about 1500 more Asian goods went to Europe by sea.
explain: Nobody in 1300 called it the Silk Road: a German geographer, Ferdinand von Richthofen, invented the name in 1877.
fact: The Silk Road was not one road but a network of land routes linking China with Central Asia, Persia, India and the Mediterranean.
fact: Few traders travelled the whole Silk Road; goods usually passed from merchant to merchant and market to market.
fact: China kept the making of silk secret for a long time, so silk was rare and costly in the West; silk is spun by silkworms, and a well-known story says monks smuggled silkworm eggs to the Byzantine Empire in the 500s.
fact: Goods crossed the deserts of Central Asia in caravans of Bactrian camels, which have two humps, go a long time without water and survive cold winters.
fact: Caravanserais, inns spaced about a day's journey apart, gave caravans safe places to rest.
fact: Cities such as Samarkand, Bukhara and Kashgar grew rich at oases on the Silk Road.
fact: In the 1200s and 1300s the Mongol Empire controlled most of the Silk Road, making it safer for traders to cross.
fact: By 1337 the Mongol world had split into separate khanates such as the Yuan, the Golden Horde and the Ilkhanate, and the Ilkhanate in Persia was falling apart.
fact: Marco Polo of Venice wrote a famous account of his journey to the court of Kublai Khan in China in the late 1200s, though some details of his book are doubted.
fact: The Silk Road carried ideas as well as goods, such as Buddhism from India to China and papermaking from China westward.
fact: The Black Death is thought to have spread from Central Asia westward along trade routes in the 1340s, its germs carried by traders, animals, goods and fleas, reaching the Crimean port of Caffa and then the Mediterranean in 1347.
fact: After about 1500 more trade between Europe and Asia went by sea, because ships could carry far more goods more cheaply, so the overland Silk Road became less important.
fact: The name "Silk Road" was invented by a German geographer, Ferdinand von Richthofen, in 1877.
exercise: What was the Silk Road? | A network of land routes linking China with the West | One long paved road that ran straight from Beijing to Rome | A sea route around Africa | A river that flowed from China to Persia
exercise: Did most traders travel the whole Silk Road? | No, goods passed from merchant to merchant | Yes, every trader went the whole way from China to Rome | Yes, but only once in their lives | No, nobody travelled on it at all
exercise: Why was silk so costly in the West? | China kept the making of silk secret | Silk could only be made in the winter | Silk was woven from threads of real gold | Rome had banned silk from its markets
exercise: Which animals carried goods across the cold deserts of Central Asia? | Bactrian camels | Elephants | Llamas | Horses only
exercise: How many humps does a Bactrian camel have? | Two | One | Three | None
exercise: What was a caravanserai? | An inn where caravans could rest | A type of silk cloth | A ship that carried spices | A tax on camels
exercise: Which city grew rich at an oasis on the Silk Road? | Samarkand | Venice | Lisbon | Amsterdam
exercise: Which empire controlled most of the Silk Road in the 1200s and 1300s? | The Mongol Empire | The Roman Empire | The Khmer Empire | The Inca Empire
exercise: Whose court did Marco Polo describe in China? | Kublai Khan's | Timur's in Samarkand | The Roman emperor's | The Ming emperor Yongle's
exercise: Where was Marco Polo from? | Venice | Beijing | Samarkand | London
exercise: Which religion travelled from India to China along the Silk Road? | Buddhism | Christianity | Islam | Judaism
exercise: Which invention travelled from China westward? | Papermaking | The steam engine | The telescope | The printing of newspapers
exercise: In which decade is the Black Death thought to have spread west along trade routes? | The 1340s | The 1640s | The 1040s | The 1940s
exercise: Why did the overland Silk Road become less important after about 1500? | More trade between Europe and Asia went by sea | The deserts of Central Asia became too hot for anyone to cross | China stopped making anything to sell | Camels died out completely
exercise: Who invented the name "Silk Road"? | Ferdinand von Richthofen, in 1877 | Marco Polo, in his book of 1298 | Kublai Khan, in 1270 | The Romans, in 100
exercise: Which of these was also traded on the Silk Road besides silk? | Horses and paper | Potatoes and maize | Lithium and cobalt | Coffee from Brazil
test: Did traders in 1300 call it the Silk Road? | No, the name was invented in 1877 | Yes, it was painted on every signpost | Yes, Marco Polo named it in his book | No, they called it the Spice Highway
test: My mate thinks the Silk Road was one big straight road like a motorway. Fix it. | It was a web of many routes between oases and markets | He is right, one paved road ran all the way from China to Rome | It was a long canal dug through the desert | It was a tunnel under the mountains
test: Why were camels better than horses for crossing deserts? | They go long without water and cope with heat and cold | Camels are faster than any horse | Horses were not allowed in deserts by the rulers who owned the oases | Camels could swim across rivers
test: Why did cities grow at oases? | Oases had water where caravans could rest and trade | Oases had gold lying on the ground | Oases were the only places where silkworms could live and spin | Oases were cooler than the mountains
test: How can trade spread a disease? | Traders, animals and goods carry germs and fleas between places | Trade makes the air in markets poisonous, and people breathe it in | Diseases travel only by sea, never with traders | Silk itself causes the illness
test: Leo starts the game in 1337 and thinks the Mongol Empire is still one giant state. Put him right. | The empire had split into rival khanates, and the Ilkhanate was falling apart | One Mongol khan still ruled everything from Korea to Hungary from his capital at Karakorum | The Mongols had all moved to Europe | The Mongols had never existed
test: Why did the Mongols make travel easier? | One power ruled most of the routes and kept them safer | They built a long railway across the whole of Asia | They gave every trader a free camel and a guard | They removed all the deserts
test: A teacher says Marco Polo's book is exact in every detail. Is that right? | No, some details are doubted, though it was very influential | Yes, every single word in it has been proved true by historians | No, the book was written in 1877 | Yes, he took photographs to prove it
test: What would happen to Samarkand if caravans stopped coming? | It would lose much of its trade and wealth | It would grow much richer | It would turn into a sea port | Nothing at all, because it never depended on trade
test: Why did sea routes take over from the Silk Road? | Ships could carry far more goods more cheaply | The sea routes became shorter after 1500 | Camels were banned by the Pope in all Christian lands | The Silk Road was flooded
test: According to a well-known story, monks smuggled something to the Byzantine Empire in the 500s. What? | Silkworm eggs | Gunpowder recipes | Sacks of potatoes | Tea plant seeds
exam: Identify the principal reason for which silk commanded a high price in the Roman world. | The Chinese long preserved the secret of its manufacture | Silk could be produced only during the winter months | The Roman state levied no customs duties upon silk | Silk was manufactured exclusively from precious metal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the routes hereinafter designated the Silk Road? | A network of overland routes along which goods passed between successive markets | A single paved highway traversed in full by every merchant from China to the Mediterranean | A maritime passage circumnavigating the continent of Africa | A canal excavated between China and the Mediterranean
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diminished importance of the overland routes after about 1500. | A greater share of trade between Europe and Asia was conveyed by sea | The deserts of Central Asia became wholly impassable owing to a change of climate | China ceased altogether the production of goods for export | The camel became extinct throughout Central Asia
exam: It is given that the designation "Silk Road" was not employed by medieval merchants. Ascertain its origin. | It was devised by the geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen in 1877 | It was devised by Marco Polo in the account of his travels | It was devised by Kublai Khan in an imperial decree | It was devised by the Roman Senate in antiquity
